lisa-design:
- Incident key = repo + caller workflow FILE + (PR number | pushed branch). The workflow file is added beyond the spec's repo+PR/branch so two workflows of one repo that both call notify don't close or reply into each other's incidents. Pinned in TestIncidentKey (changing it forgets every open incident).
- State = one JSON file in the Actions cache under ccf-notify-incident-<sha256[:16] of key>-<run_id>-<run_attempt>; restored with the prefix as restore-keys (newest entry wins). Saved only when something was posted. Cache scoping (PR merge ref / branch) keeps PR and main incidents apart; no new permissions.
- Rule (b) for the default branch no longer looks up the previous run's conclusion: the open incident replaces that dedupe (first failure opens, later failing commits reply, first pass closes). The GitHub client (PreviousConclusion) and the job's actions: read were removed as unused; the old ccf-notify-failure-* marker cache is no longer read.
- Workflow input is named `needs` (like release-finished.yml's input of the same toJSON(needs)), not the spec's example `needs-json`.
- No job-level concurrency group: a queued notify job replaced by a newer one would show as a cancelled check; the double-thread race (two runs of one incident finishing together) is documented in docs/notify.md instead.
- Detailed docs in docs/notify.md; README keeps a short summary + link and the updated caller example only (parallel stage-3 PRs edit README).
